{
 "id": "sd-emergency-truth",
 "name": "SD: An emergency department under pressure: the truth",
 "description": "Tests two effects on one flow, and an effect on a return flow. Patients waiting are treated at a rate proportional to the queue, times a linear effect of the staff on shift (staff / 12, capped at 1.3) and times a table effect of crowding (more than 20 waiting and care slows, to 0.38 of normal at 200). Staff fatigue rises with the patients treated (0.5 points per patient: the same equation and effects as treatment) and recovers 10.0% an hour; fatigue raises the rate of mistakes that send treated patients back to the queue (a table effect: half the base rate when rested, four times it when exhausted). Arrivals and staffing follow the day (unannounced), arrivals surge for weeks at a time (unannounced), and an announced era trials a new triage policy (a fast track for minor cases) that treats patients faster.",
 "time_unit": "hour",
